{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide explains how to create and contribute community adapters for the TanStack AI ecosystem."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Community adapters extend TanStack AI by integrating external services, APIs, or custom model logic. They are authored and maintained by the community and can be reused across projects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-is-a-community-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is a Community Adapter?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A community adapter is a reusable module that connects TanStack AI to an external provider or system."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common use cases include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Integrating third-party AI model providers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implementing custom inference or routing logic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exposing provider-specific tools or capabilities"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connecting to non-LLM AI services (e.g. images, embeddings, video)"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Community adapters are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not maintained by the core TanStack AI team"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and can be reused across different projects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"creating-a-community-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating a Community Adapter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow the steps below to build a well-structured, type-safe adapter."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"1-set-up-your-project","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Set up your project"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start by reviewing the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/tanstack/ai/tree/main/packages","children":[{"type":"text","value":"existing internal adapter implementations in the TanStack AI GitHub repository"}]},{"type":"text","value":". These define the expected structure, conventions, and integration patterns."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a complete, detailed reference, use the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/tanstack/ai/tree/main/packages/ai-openai","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenAI adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which is the most fully featured implementation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"2-define-model-metadata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Define model metadata"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model metadata describes each model’s capabilities and constraints and is used by TanStack AI for compatibility checks and feature selection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your metadata should define, at a minimum:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model name and identifier"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supported input and output modalities"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supported features (e.g. streaming, tools, structured output)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pricing or cost information (if available)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any provider-specific notes or limitations"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refer to the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/model-meta.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenAI adapter’s model metadata"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a concrete example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"3-define-model-capability-arrays","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Define model capability arrays"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After defining metadata, group models by supported functionality using exported arrays. These arrays allow TanStack AI to automatically select compatible models for a given task."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const OPENAI_CHAT_MODELS = [\n  // Frontier models\n  GPT5_2.name,\n  GPT5_2_PRO.name,\n  GPT5_2_CHAT.name,\n  GPT5_1.name,\n  GPT5_1_CODEX.name,\n  GPT5.name,\n  GPT5_MINI.name,\n  GPT5_NANO.name,\n  GPT5_PRO.name,\n  GPT5_CODEX.name,\n  // ...other models\n] as const\nexport const OPENAI_IMAGE_MODELS = [\n  GPT_IMAGE_1.name,\n  GPT_IMAGE_1_MINI.name,\n  DALL_E_3.name,\n  DALL_E_2.name,\n] as const\n\nexport const OPENAI_VIDEO_MODELS = [SORA2.name, SORA2_PRO.name] as const","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each array should only include models that fully support the associated functionality."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"4-define-model-provider-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Define model provider options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each model exposes a different set of configurable options. These options must be typed per model name so that users only see valid configuration options."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export type OpenAIChatModelProviderOptionsByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n  [GPT5_2_CHAT.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n  // ... repeat for each model\n}\n","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This ensures strict type safety and feature correctness at compile time."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"5-define-supported-input-modalities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Define supported input modalities"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Models typically support different input modalities (e.g. text, images, audio). These must be defined per model to prevent invalid usage."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export type OpenAIModelInputModalitiesByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: typeof GPT5_2.supports.input\n  [GPT5_2_PRO.name]: typeof GPT5_2_PRO.supports.input\n  [GPT5_2_CHAT.name]: typeof GPT5_2_CHAT.supports.input\n  //  ... repeat for each model\n}","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"6-define-model-option-fragments","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. Define model option fragments"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model options should be composed from reusable fragments rather than duplicated per model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common pattern is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base options shared by all models"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature fragments that are stitched together per model"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example (based on "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/text/text-provider-options.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenAI models"}]},{"type":"text","value":"):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export interface OpenAIBaseOptions {\n  // base options that every chat model supports\n}\n\n// Feature fragments that can be stitched per-model \n\n/**\n * Reasoning options for models  \n */\nexport interface OpenAIReasoningOptions {\n   //...\n}\n \n/**\n * Structured output options for models.\n */\nexport interface OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions {\n  //...\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Models can then opt into only the features they support:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export type OpenAIChatModelProviderOptionsByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n}","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no single correct composition; this structure should reflect the capabilities of the provider you are integrating."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"7-implement-adapter-logic","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7. Implement adapter logic"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finally, implement the adapter’s runtime logic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sending requests to the external service"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling streaming and non-streaming responses"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mapping provider responses to TanStack AI types"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enforcing model-specific options and constraints"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapters are implemented per capability, so only implement what your provider supports:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text adapter"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat adapter"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image adapter"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Embeddings adapter"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Video adapter"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refer to the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/adapters/text.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenAI adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a complete, end-to-end implementation example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"8-publish-and-submit-a-pr","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8. Publish and submit a PR"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once your adapter is complete:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Publish it as an npm package"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open a PR to the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/pulls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI repository"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add your adapter to the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/tree/main/docs/community-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Community Adapters list in the documentation"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"9-export-a-byok-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9. Export a BYOK provider"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If users can paste an API key for your adapter, export a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineByokProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" object from a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/byok"},{"type":"text","value":" subpath"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@scope/ai-acme/byok"},{"type":"text","value":"). Do not re-export it from the package main entry — that pulls your provider SDK into the browser. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-byok-<id>"},{"type":"text","value":" slug and is required. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"env"},{"type":"text","value":" to the env var "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"name"}]},{"type":"text","value":" your adapter already reads. Names only — this object is imported on the client. Relays call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getByokKey(request, acmeByok)"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai/byok/server"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineByokProvider } from \"@tanstack/ai/byok\";\n\nexport const acmeByok = defineByokProvider({\n  id: \"acme\",\n  label: \"Acme\",\n  env: \"ACME_API_KEY\",\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apps import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ acmeByok } from \"@scope/ai-acme/byok\""},{"type":"text","value":" and pass it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getByokKey(request, acmeByok)"},{"type":"text","value":" on the relay to read the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-byok-acme"},{"type":"text","value":" header."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"10-sync-documentation-configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10. Sync documentation configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After adding your adapter, run the  "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm run sync-docs-config"},{"type":"text","value":" in the root of the TanStack AI monorepo. This ensures your adapter appears correctly in the documentation navigation. Open a PR with the generated changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"11-maintain-your-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"11. Maintain your adapter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As a community adapter author, you are responsible for ongoing maintenance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tracking upstream provider API changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keeping compatibility with TanStack AI releases"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addressing issues and feedback from users"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Updating documentation when features change"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you add new features or breaking changes, open a follow-up PR to keep the docs in sync."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-a-community-adapter","text":"What is a Community Adapter?","level":2},{"id":"creating-a-community-adapter","text":"Creating a Community Adapter","level":2},{"id":"1-set-up-your-project","text":"1. Set up your project","level":3},{"id":"2-define-model-metadata","text":"2. Define model metadata","level":3},{"id":"3-define-model-capability-arrays","text":"3. Define model capability arrays","level":3},{"id":"4-define-model-provider-options","text":"4. Define model provider options","level":3},{"id":"5-define-supported-input-modalities","text":"5. Define supported input modalities","level":3},{"id":"6-define-model-option-fragments","text":"6. Define model option fragments","level":3},{"id":"7-implement-adapter-logic","text":"7. Implement adapter logic","level":3},{"id":"8-publish-and-submit-a-pr","text":"8. Publish and submit a PR","level":3},{"id":"9-export-a-byok-provider","text":"9. Export a BYOK provider","level":3},{"id":"10-sync-documentation-configuration","text":"10. Sync documentation configuration","level":3},{"id":"11-maintain-your-adapter","text":"11. Maintain your adapter","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThis guide explains how to create and contribute community adapters for the TanStack AI ecosystem.\n\nCommunity adapters extend TanStack AI by integrating external services, APIs, or custom model logic. They are authored and maintained by the community and can be reused across projects.\n\n## What is a Community Adapter?\n\nA community adapter is a reusable module that connects TanStack AI to an external provider or system.\n\nCommon use cases include:\n- Integrating third-party AI model providers\n- Implementing custom inference or routing logic\n- Exposing provider-specific tools or capabilities\n- Connecting to non-LLM AI services (e.g. images, embeddings, video)\n\nCommunity adapters are **not maintained by the core TanStack AI team**, and can be reused across different projects.\n\n## Creating a Community Adapter\n\nFollow the steps below to build a well-structured, type-safe adapter.\n\n### 1. Set up your project\n\nStart by reviewing the [existing internal adapter implementations in the TanStack AI GitHub repository](https://github.com/tanstack/ai/tree/main/packages). These define the expected structure, conventions, and integration patterns.\n\nFor a complete, detailed reference, use the [OpenAI adapter](https://github.com/tanstack/ai/tree/main/packages/ai-openai), which is the most fully featured implementation.\n\n### 2. Define model metadata\n\nModel metadata describes each model’s capabilities and constraints and is used by TanStack AI for compatibility checks and feature selection.\n\nYour metadata should define, at a minimum:\n\n- Model name and identifier\n- Supported input and output modalities\n- Supported features (e.g. streaming, tools, structured output)\n- Pricing or cost information (if available)\n- Any provider-specific notes or limitations\n\nRefer to the [OpenAI adapter’s model metadata](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/model-meta.ts) for a concrete example.\n\n### 3. Define model capability arrays \n\nAfter defining metadata, group models by supported functionality using exported arrays. These arrays allow TanStack AI to automatically select compatible models for a given task.\n\nExample:\n```typescript ignore\nexport const OPENAI_CHAT_MODELS = [\n  // Frontier models\n  GPT5_2.name,\n  GPT5_2_PRO.name,\n  GPT5_2_CHAT.name,\n  GPT5_1.name,\n  GPT5_1_CODEX.name,\n  GPT5.name,\n  GPT5_MINI.name,\n  GPT5_NANO.name,\n  GPT5_PRO.name,\n  GPT5_CODEX.name,\n  // ...other models\n] as const\nexport const OPENAI_IMAGE_MODELS = [\n  GPT_IMAGE_1.name,\n  GPT_IMAGE_1_MINI.name,\n  DALL_E_3.name,\n  DALL_E_2.name,\n] as const\n\nexport const OPENAI_VIDEO_MODELS = [SORA2.name, SORA2_PRO.name] as const\n```\nEach array should only include models that fully support the associated functionality.\n\n### 4. Define model provider options\n\nEach model exposes a different set of configurable options. These options must be typed per model name so that users only see valid configuration options.\n\nExample:\n```typescript ignore\nexport type OpenAIChatModelProviderOptionsByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n  [GPT5_2_CHAT.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n  // ... repeat for each model\n}\n\n```\nThis ensures strict type safety and feature correctness at compile time.\n\n### 5. Define supported input modalities\n\nModels typically support different input modalities (e.g. text, images, audio). These must be defined per model to prevent invalid usage.\n\nExample:\n```typescript ignore\nexport type OpenAIModelInputModalitiesByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: typeof GPT5_2.supports.input\n  [GPT5_2_PRO.name]: typeof GPT5_2_PRO.supports.input\n  [GPT5_2_CHAT.name]: typeof GPT5_2_CHAT.supports.input\n  //  ... repeat for each model\n}\n```\n\n### 6. Define model option fragments\n\nModel options should be composed from reusable fragments rather than duplicated per model.\n\nA common pattern is:\n- Base options shared by all models\n- Feature fragments that are stitched together per model\n\nExample (based on [OpenAI models](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/text/text-provider-options.ts)):\n```typescript\nexport interface OpenAIBaseOptions {\n  // base options that every chat model supports\n}\n\n// Feature fragments that can be stitched per-model \n\n/**\n * Reasoning options for models  \n */\nexport interface OpenAIReasoningOptions {\n   //...\n}\n \n/**\n * Structured output options for models.\n */\nexport interface OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions {\n  //...\n}\n```\n\n\nModels can then opt into only the features they support:\n\n```typescript ignore\nexport type OpenAIChatModelProviderOptionsByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n}\n```\n\nThere is no single correct composition; this structure should reflect the capabilities of the provider you are integrating.\n\n### 7. Implement adapter logic\n\nFinally, implement the adapter’s runtime logic.\n\nThis includes:\n- Sending requests to the external service\n- Handling streaming and non-streaming responses\n- Mapping provider responses to TanStack AI types\n- Enforcing model-specific options and constraints\n\nAdapters are implemented per capability, so only implement what your provider supports:\n\n- Text adapter\n- Chat adapter\n- Image adapter\n- Embeddings adapter\n- Video adapter\n\nRefer to the [OpenAI adapter](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/adapters/text.ts) for a complete, end-to-end implementation example.\n\n### 8. Publish and submit a PR\n\nOnce your adapter is complete:\n1. Publish it as an npm package\n2. Open a PR to the [TanStack AI repository](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/pulls)\n3. Add your adapter to the [Community Adapters list in the documentation](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/tree/main/docs/community-adapters)\n\n### 9. Export a BYOK provider\n\nIf users can paste an API key for your adapter, export a `defineByokProvider` object from a **`/byok` subpath** (`@scope/ai-acme/byok`). Do not re-export it from the package main entry — that pulls your provider SDK into the browser. `id` is the `x-byok-<id>` slug and is required. Set `env` to the env var **name** your adapter already reads. Names only — this object is imported on the client. Relays call `getByokKey(request, acmeByok)` from `@tanstack/ai/byok/server`.\n\n```typescript\nimport { defineByokProvider } from \"@tanstack/ai/byok\";\n\nexport const acmeByok = defineByokProvider({\n  id: \"acme\",\n  label: \"Acme\",\n  env: \"ACME_API_KEY\",\n});\n```\n\nApps import `{ acmeByok } from \"@scope/ai-acme/byok\"` and pass it to `getByokKey(request, acmeByok)` on the relay to read the `x-byok-acme` header.\n\n### 10. Sync documentation configuration\n\nAfter adding your adapter, run the  `pnpm run sync-docs-config` in the root of the TanStack AI monorepo. This ensures your adapter appears correctly in the documentation navigation. Open a PR with the generated changes.\n\n### 11. Maintain your adapter\n\nAs a community adapter author, you are responsible for ongoing maintenance.\n\nThis includes:\n\n- Tracking upstream provider API changes\n- Keeping compatibility with TanStack AI releases\n- Addressing issues and feedback from users\n- Updating documentation when features change\n\nIf you add new features or breaking changes, open a follow-up PR to keep the docs in sync.","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: \"Community Adapters Guide\"\nslug: /community-adapters/guide\norder: 1\ndescription: \"Build and publish a community adapter for TanStack AI — package conventions, implementing the adapter interface, and publishing to npm.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - community adapters\n  - build adapter\n  - custom adapter\n  - provider integration\n  - adapter authoring\n  - contribute\n---\n\nThis guide explains how to create and contribute community adapters for the TanStack AI ecosystem.\n\nCommunity adapters extend TanStack AI by integrating external services, APIs, or custom model logic. They are authored and maintained by the community and can be reused across projects.\n\n## What is a Community Adapter?\n\nA community adapter is a reusable module that connects TanStack AI to an external provider or system.\n\nCommon use cases include:\n- Integrating third-party AI model providers\n- Implementing custom inference or routing logic\n- Exposing provider-specific tools or capabilities\n- Connecting to non-LLM AI services (e.g. images, embeddings, video)\n\nCommunity adapters are **not maintained by the core TanStack AI team**, and can be reused across different projects.\n\n## Creating a Community Adapter\n\nFollow the steps below to build a well-structured, type-safe adapter.\n\n### 1. Set up your project\n\nStart by reviewing the [existing internal adapter implementations in the TanStack AI GitHub repository](https://github.com/tanstack/ai/tree/main/packages). These define the expected structure, conventions, and integration patterns.\n\nFor a complete, detailed reference, use the [OpenAI adapter](https://github.com/tanstack/ai/tree/main/packages/ai-openai), which is the most fully featured implementation.\n\n### 2. Define model metadata\n\nModel metadata describes each model’s capabilities and constraints and is used by TanStack AI for compatibility checks and feature selection.\n\nYour metadata should define, at a minimum:\n\n- Model name and identifier\n- Supported input and output modalities\n- Supported features (e.g. streaming, tools, structured output)\n- Pricing or cost information (if available)\n- Any provider-specific notes or limitations\n\nRefer to the [OpenAI adapter’s model metadata](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/model-meta.ts) for a concrete example.\n\n### 3. Define model capability arrays \n\nAfter defining metadata, group models by supported functionality using exported arrays. These arrays allow TanStack AI to automatically select compatible models for a given task.\n\nExample:\n```typescript ignore\nexport const OPENAI_CHAT_MODELS = [\n  // Frontier models\n  GPT5_2.name,\n  GPT5_2_PRO.name,\n  GPT5_2_CHAT.name,\n  GPT5_1.name,\n  GPT5_1_CODEX.name,\n  GPT5.name,\n  GPT5_MINI.name,\n  GPT5_NANO.name,\n  GPT5_PRO.name,\n  GPT5_CODEX.name,\n  // ...other models\n] as const\nexport const OPENAI_IMAGE_MODELS = [\n  GPT_IMAGE_1.name,\n  GPT_IMAGE_1_MINI.name,\n  DALL_E_3.name,\n  DALL_E_2.name,\n] as const\n\nexport const OPENAI_VIDEO_MODELS = [SORA2.name, SORA2_PRO.name] as const\n```\nEach array should only include models that fully support the associated functionality.\n\n### 4. Define model provider options\n\nEach model exposes a different set of configurable options. These options must be typed per model name so that users only see valid configuration options.\n\nExample:\n```typescript ignore\nexport type OpenAIChatModelProviderOptionsByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n  [GPT5_2_CHAT.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n  // ... repeat for each model\n}\n\n```\nThis ensures strict type safety and feature correctness at compile time.\n\n### 5. Define supported input modalities\n\nModels typically support different input modalities (e.g. text, images, audio). These must be defined per model to prevent invalid usage.\n\nExample:\n```typescript ignore\nexport type OpenAIModelInputModalitiesByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: typeof GPT5_2.supports.input\n  [GPT5_2_PRO.name]: typeof GPT5_2_PRO.supports.input\n  [GPT5_2_CHAT.name]: typeof GPT5_2_CHAT.supports.input\n  //  ... repeat for each model\n}\n```\n\n### 6. Define model option fragments\n\nModel options should be composed from reusable fragments rather than duplicated per model.\n\nA common pattern is:\n- Base options shared by all models\n- Feature fragments that are stitched together per model\n\nExample (based on [OpenAI models](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/text/text-provider-options.ts)):\n```typescript\nexport interface OpenAIBaseOptions {\n  // base options that every chat model supports\n}\n\n// Feature fragments that can be stitched per-model \n\n/**\n * Reasoning options for models  \n */\nexport interface OpenAIReasoningOptions {\n   //...\n}\n \n/**\n * Structured output options for models.\n */\nexport interface OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions {\n  //...\n}\n```\n\n\nModels can then opt into only the features they support:\n\n```typescript ignore\nexport type OpenAIChatModelProviderOptionsByName = {\n  [GPT5_2.name]: OpenAIBaseOptions &\n    OpenAIReasoningOptions &\n    OpenAIStructuredOutputOptions &\n    OpenAIToolsOptions &\n    OpenAIStreamingOptions &\n    OpenAIMetadataOptions\n}\n```\n\nThere is no single correct composition; this structure should reflect the capabilities of the provider you are integrating.\n\n### 7. Implement adapter logic\n\nFinally, implement the adapter’s runtime logic.\n\nThis includes:\n- Sending requests to the external service\n- Handling streaming and non-streaming responses\n- Mapping provider responses to TanStack AI types\n- Enforcing model-specific options and constraints\n\nAdapters are implemented per capability, so only implement what your provider supports:\n\n- Text adapter\n- Chat adapter\n- Image adapter\n- Embeddings adapter\n- Video adapter\n\nRefer to the [OpenAI adapter](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/packages/ai-openai/src/adapters/text.ts) for a complete, end-to-end implementation example.\n\n### 8. Publish and submit a PR\n\nOnce your adapter is complete:\n1. Publish it as an npm package\n2. Open a PR to the [TanStack AI repository](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/pulls)\n3. Add your adapter to the [Community Adapters list in the documentation](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/tree/main/docs/community-adapters)\n\n### 9. Export a BYOK provider\n\nIf users can paste an API key for your adapter, export a `defineByokProvider` object from a **`/byok` subpath** (`@scope/ai-acme/byok`). Do not re-export it from the package main entry — that pulls your provider SDK into the browser. `id` is the `x-byok-<id>` slug and is required. Set `env` to the env var **name** your adapter already reads. Names only — this object is imported on the client. Relays call `getByokKey(request, acmeByok)` from `@tanstack/ai/byok/server`.\n\n```typescript\nimport { defineByokProvider } from \"@tanstack/ai/byok\";\n\nexport const acmeByok = defineByokProvider({\n  id: \"acme\",\n  label: \"Acme\",\n  env: \"ACME_API_KEY\",\n});\n```\n\nApps import `{ acmeByok } from \"@scope/ai-acme/byok\"` and pass it to `getByokKey(request, acmeByok)` on the relay to read the `x-byok-acme` header.\n\n### 10. Sync documentation configuration\n\nAfter adding your adapter, run the  `pnpm run sync-docs-config` in the root of the TanStack AI monorepo. This ensures your adapter appears correctly in the documentation navigation. Open a PR with the generated changes.\n\n### 11. Maintain your adapter\n\nAs a community adapter author, you are responsible for ongoing maintenance.\n\nThis includes:\n\n- Tracking upstream provider API changes\n- Keeping compatibility with TanStack AI releases\n- Addressing issues and feedback from users\n- Updating documentation when features change\n\nIf you add new features or breaking changes, open a follow-up PR to keep the docs in sync."}
